%[text] # FeaEnclosure Class
%[text] **J.G. Vermond, JVsound**
%[text] Enclosure described by the results of an FEA model in Ansys.
%[text] **Namespace:** `comp`
%[text] **Superclasses:** [`comp.Enclosure`](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'doc','comp','enclosure','enclosuredoc.m')))
%%
%[text] ## Description
%[text] The FEA model imposes the velocity `DiaphragmVelocity` on the diaphragm, positive towards the front, which gives the volume velocity $ U\_{d,FEA} = v\_d S\_d $ (`VolumeVelocity`). Ansys returns three pressures over frequency: $ p\_{f,FEA} $ and $ p\_{r,FEA} $, averaged over the front and the rear surface of the diaphragm, and $ p\_{mic,FEA} $ at the microphone.
%%
%[text] ## Acoustical model
%[text] The front and the rear pressure give the loads on the diaphragm. They are shunts to the ambient pressure in the two 4-ports of `comp.Enclosure`: the rear load in $ \\mathbf{T}\_{a,e} $ (`tae`) and the front load in $ \\mathbf{T}\_{a,rad} $ (`tarad`):
%[text] ![](text:image:f3a1)
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ Z\_{a,f} = \\frac{p\_{f,FEA}}{U\_{d,FEA}}, \\quad Z\_{a,r} = -\\frac{p\_{r,FEA}}{U\_{d,FEA}} $
%[text] The minus sign follows from the rear flows, which count to the left: a diaphragm that moves to the front lowers the pressure at the rear. With port 2 of $ \\mathbf{T}\_a $ open, the diaphragm works on $ Z\_a = Z\_{a,f} + Z\_{a,r} $; the network then gives the diaphragm volume velocity $ U\_d $ of the loudspeaker system.
%[text] Both loads hold the enclosure and the radiation together. A split into an enclosure part and a radiation part, which the radiated volume velocity of a bass reflex or a horn would need, requires more results from Ansys, for example the pressure and the volume velocity at the port or the mouth.
%%
%[text] ## Pressure at the microphone
%[text] The system is linear, so the pressure at the microphone follows from the transfer $ H\_{mic} $ of the FEA model (`micTransfer`) and the diaphragm volume velocity of the system:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ H\_{mic} = \\frac{p\_{mic,FEA}}{U\_{d,FEA}}, \\quad p = H\_{mic} U\_d $
%[text] `result.Pressure` uses this pressure instead of the pressure of a point source with the radiated volume velocity, so it holds the diffraction and the directivity of the FEA model. The position of the microphone is the one in the FEA model; `MicRadius` has no effect.
%%
%[text] ## Creation
%[text] ```matlabCodeExample
%[text] obj = comp.FeaEnclosure
%[text] ```
%[text] Creates an FEA enclosure with a diaphragm velocity of 1 m/s, no result files and a default driver, `comp.Driver`, with all its parameters set to zero. Set the file names, the velocity of the FEA model and the parameters of the driver before you use the enclosure.
%%
%[text] ## Properties
%[text] The properties are listed in the order of the class definition files.
%%
%[text] ### `DiaphragmVelocity` — Velocity imposed in the FEA model
%[text] `(1,:) double` \[m/s\]
%[text] Velocity $ v\_d $ that the FEA model imposes on the diaphragm, positive towards the front: a scalar, or a value for each frequency of the files. Default: 1 m/s.
%[text] **Attributes:** `GetAccess = public`, `SetAccess = public`
%%
%[text] ### `PressureFrontFileName` — File with the front pressure
%[text] `(1,1) string`
%[text] Ansys result file with the pressure averaged over the front surface of the diaphragm. A file on the MATLAB path can be given by its name. Default: `""`.
%[text] **Attributes:** `GetAccess = public`, `SetAccess = public`
%%
%[text] ### `PressureRearFileName` — File with the rear pressure
%[text] `(1,1) string`
%[text] Ansys result file with the pressure averaged over the rear surface of the diaphragm. Default: `""`.
%[text] **Attributes:** `GetAccess = public`, `SetAccess = public`
%%
%[text] ### `PressureFarFieldFileName` — File with the pressure at the microphone
%[text] `(1,1) string`
%[text] Ansys result file with the pressure at the microphone. Default: `""`.
%[text] **Attributes:** `GetAccess = public`, `SetAccess = public`
%%
%[text] ### `Driver` — Driver in the enclosure
%[text] `(1,1) comp.Driver`
%[text] Driver that is mounted in the enclosure. Inherited from `comp.Enclosure`. Default: `comp.Driver`.
%[text] **Attributes:** `GetAccess = public`, `SetAccess = public`
%%
%[text] ### `VolumeVelocity` — Volume velocity of the FEA model
%[text] `double` \[m3/s\]
%[text] Volume velocity $ U\_{d,FEA} = v\_d S\_d $ of the diaphragm in the FEA model, with the area $ S\_d $ of `Driver`.
%[text] **Attributes:** `Dependent`
%%
%[text] ### `Frequency` — Frequencies of the files
%[text] `double` \[Hz\]
%[text] Frequencies of the file with the front pressure. The frequencies of the system must lie within them, for example with `sys.Frequency = sys.Enclosure.Frequency`.
%[text] **Attributes:** `Dependent`
%%
%[text] ## Methods
%[text:table]{"columnWidths":[200,80,-1]}
%[text] | Method | Type | Description |
%[text] | --- | --- | --- |
%[text] | `tae` | Instance | 4 × 4 transmission matrix $ \\mathbf{T}\_{a,e} $ with the rear load $ Z\_{a,r} $ as a shunt on the rear conductor. |
%[text] | `tarad` | Instance | 4 × 4 transmission matrix $ \\mathbf{T}\_{a,rad} $ with the front load $ Z\_{a,f} $ as a shunt on the front conductor. |
%[text] | `micTransfer` | Instance | Transfer $ H\_{mic} = p\_{mic}/U\_{d,FEA} $ to the microphone, interpolated to the frequencies `f`. |
%[text] | `zaFront`, `zaRear` | Instance | Loads $ Z\_{a,f} $ and $ Z\_{a,r} $ on the diaphragm. Inherited from `comp.Enclosure`. |
%[text] | `importAnsysPressureResults` | Static | Read an Ansys result file: the frequencies in Hz and the complex pressure in Pa, from the columns in MPa. |
%[text] | `zarad`, `struve` | Static | Radiation impedance of a piston and the Struve function. Inherited from `comp.Enclosure`. |
%[text:table]
%[text] Frequencies outside the range of the files give the error `FeaEnclosure:frequencyOutOfRange`.
%%
%[text] ## Examples
%%
%[text] ### Example: Sound pressure level of a front-loaded horn
%[text] Calculate a B&C 21SW152-8 in a front-loaded horn with a closed box at the rear, from the Ansys results in `toolbox\examples\helperfiles`, and plot the sound pressure level at the microphone of the FEA model. See [`feaEnclosureExample`](matlab:open(fullfile(fileparts(fileparts(which('lspsys'))),'examples','feaEnclosureExample.m'))) for more.
sys = lspsys;
sys.Enclosure = comp.FeaEnclosure;
sys.Enclosure.PressureFrontFileName = "frontloadedhorn_pfront.txt";
sys.Enclosure.PressureRearFileName = "frontloadedhorn_prear.txt";
sys.Enclosure.PressureFarFieldFileName = "frontloadedhorn_prmic.txt";
sys.Enclosure.DiaphragmVelocity = 1e-3;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Re = 6;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Le = 2.2e-3;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Qes = 0.38;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Qms = 6.4;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Fs = 32;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Sd = 1680e-4;
sys.Enclosure.Driver.Vas = 200e-3;
sys.Frequency = sys.Enclosure.Frequency;
res = sys.createResult;
semilogx(res.Frequency,res.SoundPressureLevel,LineWidth=1.5) %[output:37f5ab84]
grid on %[output:37f5ab84]
xlabel("Frequency [Hz]") %[output:37f5ab84]
ylabel("Sound pressure level [dB]") %[output:37f5ab84]
title("Sound pressure level at the microphone") %[output:37f5ab84]

